#### Description of Change

Added a `clone` method to `WebContents`. Our goal is to enable different `WebContents` instances that load pages from the same domain to share the renderer process, similar to the behavior of `window.open`. In `window.open`, the new `WebContents` reuses the opener's `SiteInstance`, allowing renderer process reuse. However, Electron's current `webContents.create` method always creates a new `SiteInstance`, resulting in a new renderer process each time.

I discovered a workaround by introducing a clone method to WebContents that creates a new instance inheriting many properties—including SiteInstance and navigation history—but not the FrameTree. The FrameTree will be loaded based on the latest navigation information when the WebContents becomes visible. This new WebContents closely matches the one created via window.open, and navigating to same-origin sites shares the same renderer process.

Example API usage:
```javascript
const webContents1 = electron.webContents.create({
  type: 'window',
  sandbox: false,
  nodeIntegration: true,
  contextIsolation: false,
});

webContents1.loadURL('https://www.qq.com');

// If you want to reuse the renderer process to open a same-origin website.
const webContents2 = webContents1.clone();
webContents2.loadURL('https://docs.qq.com');

// If you don't want to reuse the renderer process.
const webContents3 = electron.webContents.create({});
webContents3.loadURL('https://www.qq.com');

const wrapWebContentsView = (webContents) => {
  return new electron.WebContentsView({
    webContents: webContents,
    webPreferences: webContents.getLastWebPreferences(),
  });
}
```

